{
  "dom_id": "step-q332616",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
